+# Fix IPv6 SERVER_NAME development-server fallback
+
+## Goal
+
+Allow ``Flask.run()`` to derive the correct development-server address from a
+bracketed IPv6 ``SERVER_NAME`` value.
+
+## Confirmed Facts
+
+- ``Flask.run`` currently splits ``SERVER_NAME`` at its first colon in
+  ``src/flask/app.py``. This turns ``[::1]:8080`` into host ``[`` and an
+  invalid port fragment.
+- ``tests/test_basic.py::test_run_from_config`` covers the same fallback
+  behavior, including an explicitly supplied port of ``0``.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- When no host or port is supplied to ``app.run()``,
+  ``SERVER_NAME="[::1]:8080"`` must call the development server with host
+  ``"::1"`` and port ``8080``.
+- Existing hostname-and-port behavior and explicitly supplied port ``0``
+  behavior must remain unchanged.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+
+- [x] A regression case in ``test_run_from_config`` asserts that
+  ``[::1]:8080`` derives host ``::1`` and port ``8080``.
+- [x] The existing hostname and port-zero parameter cases continue to pass.
+- [x] The provisioned public test command passes.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Changes to URL routing, URL generation, request handling, or the Flask CLI.
+- Validation or normalization of arbitrary malformed ``SERVER_NAME`` values.
+
+## Key Decision
+
+- Keep parsing local to ``Flask.run`` because this fallback is the only
+  affected consumer. Do not introduce a shared parser for one small,
+  configuration-specific split.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+None. The requested behavior and compatibility constraints are complete.
+
+## Notes
+
+This is a lightweight task; a PRD is sufficient.
